  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What the City of Carrollton says about fence permits</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>According to the City of Carrollton's building inspection page, a fence permit is required for any new fence over 20 ft in length, or when you relocate or repair more than 20 ft of an existing fence. A site plan showing the proposed fence location is required. The same page says replacing boards and posts up to 20 ft does not need a permit, and it prohibits fences in visibility easements and drainage easements.</p><p>The City's handout <strong>Fence Ordinance Outline for Customers</strong> (Ordinance 3563, effective August 2013) gives a maximum of 8 ft for both rear and side yards on single-family and duplex lots. Front-yard fences are limited to no greater than 36 inches with an opacity level not exceeding 50 percent. That handout is more than ten years old, so confirm the current rule with the City before you order materials.</p><p>Do not borrow numbers from another city. Dallas allows a higher maximum than Carrollton and uses a different permit trigger, so a Dallas article does not tell you what Carrollton will approve. Check with the City and your HOA before building; in many HOAs the association can still control fence type, so get written approval first. For the Dallas side of the picture, see <a href="/fence-installation-dallas/">fence installation in Dallas</a>.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Older lots, alleys, clay and storm wind</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>A fence on a 1970s to 1990s Carrollton lot has usually been through a lot of North Texas weather. Much of the Dallas area sits on expansive clay that swells and shrinks with moisture, which is one reason posts shift and sections lean. The National Weather Service counts a thunderstorm with 58 mph winds as severe, so a fence that lasted two decades can still lose a section in one spring storm.</p><p>The repair-or-replace question usually comes down to how many posts have failed. One Dallas fence company advertises $25 to $60 per board installed, $300 to $800 for straightening a 10 to 30 ft leaning section, and $1,200 to $3,500 for a full panel rebuild after wind (20 to 50 ft). Because the City's permit line is 20 ft, a big repair may need a permit even though it is not a new fence. See <a href="/fence-repair-dallas/">fence repair</a> for the wider picture.</p><p>On alley-backed lots, ask the contractor to check the plat or survey for easements before posts go in, because the City prohibits fences in visibility and drainage easements. Texas law requires you or your contractor to call 811 two business days before digging, and for a hired contractor the 811 call is the contractor's responsibility. Do not skip it because the yard is your own.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Pricing, gates and who to hire</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Advertised prices vary widely, so read them as one firm's range and not as a quote. One DFW firm advertises $18 to $28 per linear foot installed for a standard 6 ft cedar privacy fence, while another advertises $30 to $60 for mid-range cedar at the same height. The first firm says board-on-board and shadow box styles typically add $2 to $5 per linear foot over a basic dog-ear fence. For iron, one DFW firm advertises $30 to $45 per linear foot for standard tubular steel; see <a href="/fence-cost-dallas/">fence costs</a> and <a href="/iron-fence-dallas/">iron fences</a>.</p><p>Gates are a separate line. One Dallas-area firm advertises that most residential automatic gate installations in the Dallas area run $3,500 to $10,000, and the same firm lists lower figures on another page, so treat any single number carefully. Opener power wiring is electrical work; ask the installer who holds the electrical licence. For a gate that has simply stopped working, start with <a href="/gate-repair-dallas/">gate repair</a>.</p><p>Texas does not license fence contractors at the state level, and the City pages we read do not say whether Carrollton registers them. Ask the City, then ask each contractor for proof of insurance and a written scope that names the height, the materials and who pulls any permit. Neighboring Richardson, for example, requires fence contractors to be registered, so the rules differ city by city; see <a href="/fence-company-richardson/">Richardson</a> or <a href="/fence-company-plano/">Plano</a> if your job is across the line.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#F6F3EE;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 10px;">Do I need a permit for a fence in Carrollton?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">According to the City of Carrollton, a permit is required for any new fence over 20 ft in length, or when you relocate or repair more than 20 ft of an existing fence, and a site plan is required. Replacing boards and posts up to 20 ft does not need one. Confirm with the City and your HOA before work starts.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 10px;">How tall can a back fence be in Carrollton?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The City's Fence Ordinance Outline for Customers (Ordinance 3563, effective 2013) gives a maximum of 8 ft for rear and side yards on single-family and duplex lots, and 36 inches with no more than 50 percent opacity in the front yard. The handout is old, so check the current rule with the City. Your HOA may set a lower limit or control the fence style.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 10px;">Does an alley-backed lot change what I can build?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">The City prohibits fences in visibility easements and drainage easements, and alley-backed lots often have utility or drainage strips near the rear line. This page cannot say what the City requires for an alley-side gate, so ask the City. Ask your contractor to check the plat or survey before posts go in, and make sure 811 is called.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 10px;">Who pays when a fence is shared with a neighbor?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">This is general information from TexasLawHelp, not legal advice. It says that if both neighbors use a fence straddling the boundary line, both own it and share the cost of repairing and replacing it, while a fence wholly on one lot belongs to that owner. Talk to your neighbor and get the boundary confirmed before you order work.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
